INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

A round room, whitewashed. A kettle ticks on an iron stove. Rain slides down the glass in slow threads. Five places are set at a table built for four.

GRANDMOTHER ILONA (80s, shawl, hands folded) watches the empty fifth chair. Her grandson TOBY (20s, damp jacket) stirs his soup and does not drink it. His wife NELL (20s, pregnant, cardigan buttoned wrong) counts the spoons. NELL'S BROTHER RAFE (30s, unshaven) pours whisky into his tea. And the housekeeper, MRS. PATTERSON (60s, apron), sets down bread with exaggerated care.

The lamp above the table dims. Brightens.

GRANDMOTHER ILONA
Toby, the bread.

TOBY
Thank you, Gran.

He doesn't take any. Nell slides the board toward him with one finger.

NELL
Rafe, you were out at the rocks today.

RAFE
Checking the lines.

NELL
Find anything?

RAFE
Nothing worth mentioning, Nell.

Mrs. Patterson sets a sixth bowl at the fifth place. Nobody comments. The kettle goes quiet. The soup steams across the empty chair.

GRANDMOTHER ILONA
Mrs. Patterson, thank you.

MRS. PATTERSON
He always liked the leeks.

Toby puts down his spoon. Rafe turns his cup in a slow circle. Far above them, the great lamp grinds round, and its beam crosses the window once, white, then gone.
